    - Let the area of $\\triangle ABC$ be denoted as $S(ABC)$. To find the area of $\\triangle AC_{1}B_{2}$, note that it is part of $\\triangle ABC$.\n    - Using the midpoint and line intersection properties, we know $S\\left(A C_{1} B_{2}\\right) = S\\left(AK B_{2}\\right) + S\\left(A C_{1} K\\right)$:\n      \\[\n      S(A C_{1} B_{2}) = \\frac{AC_{1} \\cdot AB_{2}}{AC \\cdot AB} S(ABC) = \\frac{c_{1} \\cdot r \\cdot s}{2b}\n      \\]\n\n4. **Further simplify using other smaller triangles area relations:**\n    - For $\\triangle AK B_{2}$:\n      \\[\n      S\\left(A K B_{2} \\right) = \\frac{c_{1} \\cdot r}{2}\n      \\]\n    - For $\\triangle A C_{1} K$:\n      \\[\n      S\\left(A C_{1} K\\right) = \\frac{c \\cdot r}{4}\n      \\]\n\n5. **Combine and express relations in equations:**\n    - Combining these areas and using previously deduced properties:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{c_{1} \\cdot r \\cdot s}{2b} = \\frac{c_{1} \\cdot r}{2} + \\frac{c \\cdot r}{4}\n      \\]\n\n6. **Simplify the derived equations:**\n    - From the equation above, simplify to get direct relation:\n      \\[\n      (a - b + c)c_{1} = b c\n      \\]\n    - Similarly, analyzing $\\triangle AB_{1}C_{2}$ leads to:\n      \\[\n      (a + b - c)b_{1} = b c\n      \\]\n    - We then derived from areas equality that:\n      \\[\n      b_{1}c_{1} = b c\n      \\]\n\n7. **Apply the Cosine Law to solve for angle \\( \\angle BAC \\):**\n    - Combine all relationships to form side relation:\n      \\[\n      a^2 - (b - c)^2 = b c\n      \\]\n    - This translates into cosine law applicability:\n      \\[\n      \\frac{b^2 + c^2 - a^2}{2 b c} = \\cos(\\angle BAC) = \\frac{1}{2} \n      \\]\n    - Which simplifies to:\n      \\[\n      \\cos(\\angle BAC) = \\frac{1}{2} \\implies \\angle BAC = 60^\\circ\n      \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, the measure of $\\angle CAB$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{60^\\circ}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem":