d D.\n\nAlternatively, note that girls with different neighbors (D) are those where their left and right neighbors are different. Each such girl has either a right neighbor B or R. So D_B + D_R = D.\n\nBut we also know that the total number of times B appears as a right neighbor is 17, which includes both S_B and D_B. Similarly, R appears 13 times.\n\nWe also know that the number of girls with same neighbors (S) is 30 - D.\n\nBut how else can we relate these variables?\n\nWait, consider that each girl with same neighbors (S) has both neighbors the same. If their right neighbor is B, they contribute to S_B; if R, to S_R.\n\nSimilarly, each girl with different neighbors (D) has neighbors of different colors. If their right neighbor is B, they contribute to D_B; if R, to D_R.\n\nNow, consider the total number of adjacent pairs of the same color. For example, the number of RR pairs and BB pairs.\n\nIn a circle, the number of RR pairs plus BB pairs plus RB pairs plus BR pairs equals 30.\n\nBut RB and BR are the transitions. The number of transitions T is equal to the number of RB plus BR pairs. Since it's a circle, T must be even.\n\nBut the number of RR pairs is equal to the number of R girls minus the number of R blocks. Similarly, the number of BB pairs is equal to the number of B girls minus the number of B blocks. But this might complicate things.\n\nAlternatively, think of each girl with same neighbors as being part of a run of at least two of the same color. The girls in the middle of a run have same neighbors, while those at the ends have different neighbors.\n\nBut perhaps we can use the fact that the number of girls with same neighbors (S) is equal to the total number of girls minus twice the number of transitions (since each transition contributes two girls with different neighbors). As before, S=30-2T, D=2T.\n\nBut how does this help?\n\nWe know that N_B=17, which is the total number of times B is a right neighbor. This includes S_B and D_B.\n\nSimilarly, N_R=13, which includes S_R and D_R.\n\nBut Y = S_B + D_R\n\nWe need to find Y.\n\nBut S_B = N_B - D_B =17 - D_B\n\nD_R = N_R - S_R =13 - S_R\n\nBut S_R = S - S_B = (30 - D) - S_B\n\nSo D_R =13 - (30 - D - S_B)=13 -30 + D + S_B= D + S_B -17\n\nSubstitute into Y:\n\nY= S_B + (D + S_B -17)=2S_B + D -17\n\nBut we need another relation between S_B and D.\n\nAlternatively, since D=2T and S=30-2T, but I don't see the connection.\n\nWait, maybe consider that each time there is a transition from B to R, the last B in the B block has right neighbor R and is part of D, contributing to D_R. Similarly, the first R in the R block has right neighbor R and is part of D, contributing to D_R.\n\nSimilarly, transitions from R to B contribute to D_B.\n\nSpecifically, for each B to R transition:\n\n- Last B (right neighbor R) contributes to D_R.\n\n- First R (right neighbor R) contributes to D_R.\n\nSo per B to R transition, we get two contributions to D_R.\n\nSimilarly, for each R to B transition:\n\n- Last R (right neighbor B) contributes to D_B.\n\n- First B (right neighbor B) contributes to D_B.\n\nThus, the number of D_R is twice the number of B to R transitions.\n\nSimilarly, the number of D_B is twice the number of R to B transitions.\n\nLet\u2019s denote U as the number of B to R transitions and V as the number of R to B transitions. Since it's a circle, U=V.\n\nBut the total number of transitions T=U+V=2U.\n\nTherefore, D=2T=4U.\n\nAlso, D_R=2U and D_B=2V=2U.\n\nTherefore, D_R=D_B=2U.\n\nBut since D_R=D_B, and D_R + D_B=D=4U, then indeed D_R=D_B=2U.\n\nNow, substitute into Y= S_B + D_R.\n\nBut S_B=17 - D_B=17 -2U.\n\nD_R=2U.\n\nThus, Y=(17 -2U) +2U=17.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the number of transitions U, Y=17.\n\nWow, so this shows that Y is always 17, no matter how the girls are arranged. Because the contributions from S_B and D_R cancel out the variable U. Therefore, the number of \"yes\" answers is always equal to the number of B girls, which is 17.\n\nBut let's verify this with another example. Suppose we have three transitions. Wait, no, transitions must be even. Let's say four transitions. Wait, we already did two and four and six transitions, all gave Y=17.\n\nTherefore, the answer must be 17.\n\nBut wait, the problem says \"how many girls could have answered affirmatively.\" It's asking for the possible number, not necessarily a fixed number. But according to this analysis, it's always 17. Is that possible?\n\nBut wait, the key insight was that Y=17 regardless of the arrangement. Because N_B=17, and through the equations, Y=17.\n\nBut let's check if there's a mistake in the reasoning. For example, suppose all girls are B. Wait, but there are 13 R girls. So that's impossible. But suppose we have all B's except one R. Then N_B=29, but since there are 17 B's, impossible. So in our problem, N_B is fixed at 17 because there are 17 B girls, each B girl is the right neighbor of exactly one girl. Therefore, N_B=17.\n\nSimilarly, N_R=13.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the arrangement, the total number of \"yes\" answers Y is equal to N_B (17) because:\n\nY = S_B + D_R\n\nBut S_B =17 - D_B\n\nD_R=13 - S_R=13 - (S - S_B)=13 - (30 - D - S_B)=13 -30 + D + S_B= D + S_B -17\n\nTherefore:\n\nY= S_B + D_R= S_B + D + S_B -17=2S_B + D -17\n\nBut since D=2T, and S_B=17 - D_B=17 - (D - D_R)=17 - D + D_R\n\nBut substituting D_R= Y - S_B into this gives:\n\nS_B=17 - D + (Y - S_B)\n\n2S_B=17 - D + Y\n\nBut Y=2S_B + D -17\n\nSubstitute Y:\n\n2S_B=17 - D +2S_B + D -17\n\nWhich simplifies to 0=0.\n\nThis suggests that the equation holds for any S_B and D, but in reality, due to the constraints of the problem, Y must always equal 17.\n\nBecause N_B is fixed at 17, and Y is calculated as the sum of S_B and D_R, which through the equations, must equal 17.\n\nTherefore, regardless of the arrangement of the girls, the number of \"yes\" answers must always be 17.\n\nHence, the answer is 17.", "deepseek_solution": "The number of girls who could have answered affirmatively is determined by analyzing the constraints and structure of the problem. Here's the systematic breakdown:\n\n1. **Setup**: There are 30 girls (13 red, 17 blue) arranged in a circle. Each girl is asked if the girl to her right is in blue. Only those with both neighbors of the same color answered correctly; others answered incorrectly.\n\n2. **Key Observations**:\n   - **Neighbor Colors**: A girl answers correctly if her left and right neighbors are the same color (truthful answer). If her neighbors differ, her answer is the opposite (incorrect).\n   - **Right Neighbors**: Each girl's right neighbor is unique, forming a cyclic dependency. The total number of times blue (B) is a right neighbor equals the number of B girls (17), and similarly for red (R) with 13.\n\n3. **Affirmative Answers**:\n   - **Correct \"Yes\"**: Girls with same-colored neighbors and a blue right neighbor contribute truthful \"yes\" answers.\n   - **Incorrect \"Yes\"**: Girls with different-colored neighbors and a red right neighbor lie and say \"yes\".\n\n4. **Mathematical Derivation**:\n   - Let \\( Y \\) = total \"yes\" answers.\n   - \\( Y = \\text{(Correct \"yes\")} + \\text{(Incorrect \"yes\")} \\).\n   - Correct \"yes\" = Girls with same neighbors and right neighbor B (\\( S_B \\)).\n   - Incorrect \"yes\" = Girls with different neighbors and right neighbor R (\\( D_R \\)).\n   - Total B right neighbors (\\( N_B \\)) = 17 = \\( S_B + D_B \\).\n   - Total R right neighbors (\\( N_R \\)) = 13 = \\( S_R + D_R \\).\n   - Girls with same neighbors (\\( S \\)) = 30 - D (different neighbors).\n\n   Through substitutions and recognizing that transitions between colors balance out, it simplifies to **\\( Y = 17 \\)** regardless of arrangement.\n\n**Final Answer**: \\boxed{17}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are given that there are 30 girls divided into two groups: 13 in red dresses and 17 in blue dresses. These girls are standing in a circle around a New Year\u2019s tree, and each girl was asked if her neighbor on the right was in a blue dress. The correct affirmative answers were given only by the girls standing between two girls of the same dress color.\n\n1. **Determine the possible configurations for each girl:**\n   - Any chosen girl has neighbors that can be in the following configurations:\n     1. Both neighbors in blue dresses (blue-blue).\n     2. Left neighbor in blue and right neighbor in red (blue-red).\n     3. Left neighbor in red and right neighbor in blue (red-blue).\n     4. Both neighbors in red dresses (red-red).\n\n2. **Analyze the correctness of answers based on configurations:**\n   - A girl correctly answers \"yes\" if her neighbor to the right is in a blue dress.\n   - Hence, the girl can answer \"yes\" correctly in the configurations blue-blue and blue-red. \n\n3. **Link configurations to the dresses of the girls who answer \"yes\":**\n   - For a girl to correctly answer \"yes,\" her neighbor on the left must be in a blue dress. Therefore, only those girls who have a left neighbor in a blue dress will be able to give a correct affirmative answer.\n\n4. **Count the number of such girls:**\n   - Since there are 17 girls in blue dresses and each girl in a blue dress will have her right-hand neighbor to be the girl who is answering the question, the number of girls who can correctly answer \"yes\" is precisely the number of girls in blue dresses.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - Because there are 17 girls in blue dresses who, each having a neighbor to the left in a blue dress, can correctly answer that her right-hand neighbor is in a blue dress, the number of girls giving a correct affirmative answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{17}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "If \\( z_{1} \\) and \\( z_{2} \\) are two non-zero complex numbers with equal modulus, then the necessary and sufficient condition for \\( \\left(z_{1} + z_{2}\\right)^{2} = \\lambda^{2} z_{1} z_{2} \\) is \\( \\left|z_{1} + z_{2}\\right| = \\lambda \\left|z_{k}\\right| \\) where \\( \\lambda \\in \\mathbf{R}^{+} \\) and \\( k = 1, 2 \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out why the equation (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082 is equivalent to |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096| given that z\u2081 and z\u2082 have the same modulus. Let me start by understanding the problem step by step.\n\nFirst, both z\u2081 and z\u2082 are non-zero complex numbers with equal modulus. That means |z\u2081| = |z\u2082|. Let's denote this common modulus as r, so |z\u2081| = |z\u2082| = r. The problem states that the necessary and sufficient condition for (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082 is that |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096|, where \u03bb is a positive real number and k is either 1 or 2. Since |z\u2081| and |z\u2082| are equal, it doesn't matter which k we choose; \u03bb |z\u2096| would just be \u03bb r in either case.\n\nSo, the goal is to show that these two equations are equivalent under the condition that |z\u2081| = |z\u2082|. Let's break down both sides of the given equation and see how they relate to the modulus condition.\n\nStarting with the equation (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082. Let me expand the left-hand side (LHS):\n\n(z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = z\u2081\u00b2 + 2 z\u2081 z\u2082 + z\u2082\u00b2.\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\nz\u2081\u00b2 + 2 z\u2081 z\u2082 + z\u2082\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082.\n\nSubtracting \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082 from both sides gives:\n\nz\u2081\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) z\u2081 z\u2082 + z\u2082\u00b2 = 0.\n\nHmm, this is a quadratic equation in terms of z\u2081 and z\u2082. Maybe it would help to factor this expression. Let me try to factor it:\n\nz\u2081\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) z\u2081 z\u2082 + z\u2082\u00b2.\n\nIf I consider this as a quadratic in z\u2081, it's z\u2081\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) z\u2082 z\u2081 + z\u2082\u00b2. The discriminant would be [(2 - \u03bb\u00b2) z\u2082]\u00b2 - 4 * 1 * z\u2082\u00b2.\n\nCalculating the discriminant:\n\n(2 - \u03bb\u00b2)\u00b2 z\u2082\u00b2 - 4 z\u2082\u00b2 = z\u2082\u00b2 [(2 - \u03bb\u00b2)\u00b2 - 4].\n\nLet me compute the term inside the brackets:\n\n(2 - \u03bb\u00b2)\u00b2 - 4 = (4 - 4 \u03bb\u00b2 + \u03bb\u2074) - 4 = \u03bb\u2074 - 4 \u03bb\u00b2.\n\nSo the discriminant is z\u2082\u00b2 (\u03bb\u2074 - 4 \u03bb\u00b2) = z\u2082\u00b2 \u03bb\u00b2 (\u03bb\u00b2 - 4).\n\nFor the quadratic to factor nicely, the discriminant should be a perfect square. However, since we are dealing with complex numbers, maybe factoring isn't the best approach here. Let me think differently.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I can divide both sides of the original equation by z\u2081 z\u2082. Let's try that. Starting from:\n\n(z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082.\n\nDivide both sides by z\u2081 z\u2082 (since z\u2081 and z\u2082 are non-zero, this is allowed):\n\n(z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 / (z\u2081 z\u2082) = \u03bb\u00b2.\n\nLet me write this as [(z\u2081 + z\u2082)/\u221a(z\u2081 z\u2082)]\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2. Wait, but square roots in complex numbers can be tricky. Maybe instead, express the left side as a ratio squared:\n\n[(z\u2081 + z\u2082)/\u221a(z\u2081 z\u2082)]\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2.\n\nBut perhaps a better approach is to express this ratio as (z\u2081/z\u2082 + 1)\u00b2 / (z\u2081/z\u2082). Let me set w = z\u2081/z\u2082. Since z\u2081 and z\u2082 have the same modulus, |w| = |z\u2081| / |z\u2082| = 1. Therefore, w is a complex number on the unit circle, so w = e^{i\u03b8} for some real \u03b8.\n\nSubstituting w into the equation, we have:\n\n(z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082\n\nDivide both sides by z\u2082\u00b2:\n\n(z\u2081/z\u2082 + 1)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 (z\u2081/z\u2082)\n\nSo (w + 1)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 w.\n\nExpanding the left side:\n\nw\u00b2 + 2w + 1 = \u03bb\u00b2 w.\n\nRearranging terms:\n\nw\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) w + 1 = 0.\n\nThis is a quadratic equation in w. The solutions are:\n\nw = [ (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2) \u00b1 sqrt( (2 - \u03bb\u00b2)^2 - 4 * 1 * 1 ) ] / 2\n\nCompute the discriminant:\n\n(2 - \u03bb\u00b2)^2 - 4 = 4 - 4 \u03bb\u00b2 + \u03bb\u2074 - 4 = \u03bb\u2074 - 4 \u03bb\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2(\u03bb\u00b2 - 4).\n\nTherefore,\n\nw = [ (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2) \u00b1 sqrt(\u03bb\u00b2(\u03bb\u00b2 - 4)) ] / 2\n\n= [ (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2) \u00b1 \u03bb sqrt(\u03bb\u00b2 - 4) ] / 2.\n\nSince \u03bb is a positive real number, sqrt(\u03bb\u00b2) is \u03bb. But sqrt(\u03bb\u00b2 - 4) requires that \u03bb\u00b2 - 4 \u2265 0, so \u03bb \u2265 2. Otherwise, we have a square root of a negative number, which would make w complex. However, since w is already a complex number on the unit circle, that's okay. So even if \u03bb < 2, we can have complex roots.\n\nBut perhaps we need to relate this to the modulus condition |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096| = \u03bb r.\n\nLet me compute |z\u2081 + z\u2082|. Since |z\u2081| = |z\u2082| = r, we can use the formula for the modulus of a sum:\n\n|z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2 = |z\u2081|\u00b2 + |z\u2082|\u00b2 + 2 Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) = r\u00b2 + r\u00b2 + 2 Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) = 2 r\u00b2 + 2 Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}).\n\nBut we are told that |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb r. Therefore,\n\n|z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 r\u00b2.\n\nThus,\n\n2 r\u00b2 + 2 Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) = \u03bb\u00b2 r\u00b2\n\nDivide both sides by r\u00b2 (since r \u2260 0):\n\n2 + 2 Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) / r\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2.\n\nBut z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082} is a complex number whose modulus is |z\u2081| |z\u2082| = r\u00b2. Let me denote z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082} = r\u00b2 e^{i\u03b8}, where \u03b8 is the argument of z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}. Then Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) = r\u00b2 cos\u03b8.\n\nSubstituting back:\n\n2 + 2 (r\u00b2 cos\u03b8) / r\u00b2 = 2 + 2 cos\u03b8 = \u03bb\u00b2.\n\nTherefore,\n\n2 + 2 cos\u03b8 = \u03bb\u00b2\n\n=> cos\u03b8 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2.\n\nSo the angle between z\u2081 and z\u2082 (since \u03b8 is the angle between z\u2081 and z\u2082) must satisfy cos\u03b8 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2. However, cos\u03b8 must be in the range [-1, 1], so:\n\n(\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2 \u2208 [-1, 1]\n\nMultiply by 2:\n\n\u03bb\u00b2 - 2 \u2208 [-2, 2]\n\nAdd 2:\n\n\u03bb\u00b2 \u2208 [0, 4]\n\nBut since \u03bb is a positive real number, \u03bb\u00b2 \u2208 (0, 4], so \u03bb \u2208 (0, 2].\n\nWait, this suggests that \u03bb must be between 0 and 2. But earlier, when solving the quadratic for w, we saw that if \u03bb < 2, the discriminant becomes negative. However, even with \u03bb < 2, the quadratic in w can still have complex roots. Let me check the consistency here.\n\nSo, from the modulus condition, we get that \u03bb is in (0, 2], but from the quadratic equation for w, if \u03bb < 2, sqrt(\u03bb\u00b2 - 4) becomes sqrt(negative number), so the roots for w would be complex. But since w is on the unit circle, this is acceptable.\n\nWait, but let's recall that we had:\n\nw\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) w + 1 = 0.\n\nIf \u03bb < 2, then the discriminant is negative, leading to complex roots. However, since w is a complex number on the unit circle, that's fine. Let me check if these roots indeed lie on the unit circle.\n\nSuppose \u03bb < 2. Then the roots are:\n\nw = [ (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2) \u00b1 i \u03bb sqrt(4 - \u03bb\u00b2) ] / 2.\n\nLet me compute |w|\u00b2:\n\n|w|\u00b2 = [ (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2 ]\u00b2 + [ \u03bb sqrt(4 - \u03bb\u00b2)/2 ]\u00b2\n\n= (\u03bb\u2074 - 4 \u03bb\u00b2 + 4)/4 + (\u03bb\u00b2 (4 - \u03bb\u00b2))/4\n\n= (\u03bb\u2074 -4\u03bb\u00b2 +4 +4 \u03bb\u00b2 - \u03bb\u2074)/4\n\n= (4)/4 = 1.\n\nTherefore, |w| = 1, which is consistent with |z\u2081| = |z\u2082|, so w is indeed on the unit circle. So even when \u03bb < 2, the roots are valid. Therefore, the modulus condition |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb r is possible for \u03bb \u2208 (0, 2], but in the original problem statement, \u03bb is in R\u207a, which includes all positive real numbers. However, according to the modulus condition, \u03bb cannot exceed 2. Wait, there might be a conflict here.\n\nWait, the problem statement says \u03bb \u2208 R\u207a, which is positive real numbers. But from our analysis, \u03bb must be in (0, 2]. Therefore, maybe the problem statement should have \u03bb \u2208 R\u207a with \u03bb \u2264 2? Or perhaps I made a mistake somewhere.\n\nWait, let's re-examine the modulus condition. |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096|. Since |z\u2081 + z\u2082| can be as large as |z\u2081| + |z\u2082| = 2r (when z\u2081 and z\u2082 are in the same direction), and as small as | |z\u2081| - |z\u2082| | = 0 (when they are in opposite directions). But since z\u2081 and z\u2082 have equal modulus, |z\u2081 + z\u2082| ranges from 0 to 2r. Therefore, \u03bb can range from 0 to 2, but since \u03bb is in R\u207a, \u03bb \u2208 (0, 2]. So the original problem statement might have an implicit assumption that \u03bb is in (0, 2].\n\nBut let's get back to the main equation. The problem states that (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082 is equivalent to |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096|, given |z\u2081| = |z\u2082|.\n\nWe need to show that one implies the other and vice versa.\n\nFirst, assume that (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082. Then, we need to show that |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096|.\n\nStarting with (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082.\n\nTake modulus on both sides:\n\n| (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 | = | \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082 |.\n\nLeft side: |z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2.\n\nRight side: \u03bb\u00b2 |z\u2081| |z\u2082| = \u03bb\u00b2 r\u00b2 (since |z\u2081| = |z\u2082| = r).\n\nTherefore,\n\n|z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 r\u00b2\n\nTaking square roots (since modulus is non-negative):\n\n|z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb r = \u03bb |z\u2096|.\n\nHence, if (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082 holds, then |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096| must hold. So that's the forward direction.\n\nNow for the reverse: Assume that |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096| = \u03bb r. Then, we need to show that (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082.\n\nGiven |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb r, then |z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 r\u00b2. But |z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2 = (z\u2081 + z\u2082)(\\overline{z\u2081} + \\overline{z\u2082}) = |z\u2081|\u00b2 + |z\u2082|\u00b2 + z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082} + \\overline{z\u2081} z\u2082 = 2 r\u00b2 + 2 Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}).\n\nSo,\n\n2 r\u00b2 + 2 Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) = \u03bb\u00b2 r\u00b2.\n\nDividing both sides by r\u00b2:\n\n2 + 2 Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) / r\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2.\n\nBut z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082} has modulus r\u00b2, so Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) = r\u00b2 cos\u03b8 where \u03b8 is the angle between z\u2081 and z\u2082. Therefore,\n\n2 + 2 cos\u03b8 = \u03bb\u00b2\n\n=> cos\u03b8 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2.\n\nNow, we need to connect this back to the equation (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082.\n\nLet me compute (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2:\n\n(z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = z\u2081\u00b2 + 2 z\u2081 z\u2082 + z\u2082\u00b2.\n\nCompare this to \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082. So, the equation is:\n\nz\u2081\u00b2 + 2 z\u2081 z\u2082 + z\u2082\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082.\n\nWhich simplifies to:\n\nz\u2081\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) z\u2081 z\u2082 + z\u2082\u00b2 = 0.\n\nAlternatively, divide both sides by z\u2081 z\u2082 (since they are non-zero):\n\n(z\u2081/z\u2082) + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) + (z\u2082/z\u2081) = 0.\n\nLet w = z\u2081/z\u2082 as before. Then, since |w| = 1, we have \\overline{w} = 1/w. So,\n\nw + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) + 1/w = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by w:\n\nw\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) w + 1 = 0.\n\nWhich is the same quadratic equation we had before. The solutions to this quadratic are the w such that this equation holds.\n\nBut from the modulus condition, we have cos\u03b8 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2, which relates to the argument of w, since w = e^{i\u03b8}. So, Re(w) = cos\u03b8 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2.\n\nTherefore, if we can show that this implies w\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2)w + 1 = 0, then the original equation (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082 holds.\n\nBut since w = e^{i\u03b8}, let's compute w\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2)w + 1:\n\nw\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2)w + 1 = e^{i2\u03b8} + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2)e^{i\u03b8} + 1.\n\nBut we know that cos\u03b8 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2. Let's express this in terms of \u03b8.\n\nGiven cos\u03b8 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2, then let's use Euler's formula:\n\ne^{i\u03b8} + e^{-i\u03b8} = 2 cos\u03b8 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2).\n\nSo,\n\ne^{i\u03b8} + e^{-i\u03b8} = \u03bb\u00b2 - 2.\n\nLet me rearrange this:\n\ne^{i2\u03b8} + 1 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2) e^{i\u03b8}.\n\nMultiply both sides by e^{i\u03b8}:\n\ne^{i3\u03b8} + e^{i\u03b8} = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2) e^{i2\u03b8}.\n\nWait, this seems a bit convoluted. Maybe another approach. Let's substitute w = e^{i\u03b8} into the quadratic equation.\n\nWe have w\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2)w + 1 = 0.\n\nMultiply by w^{-1}:\n\nw + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) + w^{-1} = 0.\n\nBut w + w^{-1} = 2 Re(w) = 2 cos\u03b8.\n\nSo,\n\n2 cos\u03b8 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) = 0.\n\nBut from earlier, we have 2 cos\u03b8 = \u03bb\u00b2 - 2. Therefore,\n\n(\u03bb\u00b2 - 2) + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2) = 0.\n\nWhich simplifies to 0 = 0. Therefore, the equation holds true. This means that if |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb r, then (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082. Therefore, the reverse direction holds.\n\nTherefore, both directions are satisfied: the equation (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082 holds if and only if |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096|.\n\nWait, but the problem statement says \"the necessary and sufficient condition for (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082 is |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb |z\u2096|\". So, we've shown both directions. Therefore, the conclusion is correct.\n\nLet me summarize the steps again to ensure no gaps.\n\nForward Direction: Assume (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 z\u2081 z\u2082. Take modulus squared on both sides to get |z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 |z\u2081| |z\u2082|. Since |z\u2081| = |z\u2082| = r, this gives |z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 r\u00b2, so |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb r = \u03bb |z\u2096|.\n\nReverse Direction: Assume |z\u2081 + z\u2082| = \u03bb r. Then |z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2 = \u03bb\u00b2 r\u00b2. Expanding |z\u2081 + z\u2082|\u00b2 gives 2 r\u00b2 + 2 Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) = \u03bb\u00b2 r\u00b2. Solving for Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) gives Re(z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082}) = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2 * r\u00b2. Expressing z\u2081 \\overline{z\u2082} as r\u00b2 e^{i\u03b8}, we find cos\u03b8 = (\u03bb\u00b2 - 2)/2. Then, substituting back into the equation (z\u2081 + z\u2082)\u00b2, we relate this to the quadratic equation in w = z\u2081/z\u2082, which is satisfied when w\u00b2 + (2 - \u03bb\u00b2)w + 1 = 0. Hence, the original equation holds.\n\nTherefore, both conditions are indeed equivalent under the given circumstances.", "deepseek_solution": "The necessary and sufficient condition for \\((z_1 + z_2)^2 = \\lambda^2 z_1 z_2\\) given that \\(z_1\\) and \\(z_2\\) are non-zero complex numbers with equal modulus is derived as follows:\n\n**Forward Direction (\\( \\Rightarrow \\))**:\n1. Assume \\((z_1 + z_2)^2 = \\lambda^2 z_1 z_2\\).\n2. Take the modulus on both sides:  \n   \\[\n   |z_1 + z_2|^2 = |\\lambda^2 z_1 z_2| = \\lambda^2 |z_1||z_2|.\n   \\]\n3. Since \\(|z_1| = |z_2| = r\\), substitute to get:  \n   \\[\n   |z_1 + z_2|^2 = \\lambda^2 r^2 \\implies |z_1 + z_2| = \\lambda r = \\lambda |z_k|.\n   \\]\n\n**Reverse Direction (\\( \\Leftarrow \\))**:\n1. Assume \\(|z_1 + z_2| = \\lambda r\\).\n2. Square both sides:  \n   \\[\n   |z_1 + z_2|^2 = \\lambda^2 r^2.\n   \\]\n3. Expand the left-hand side using inner products:  \n   \\[\n   |z_1|^2 + |z_2|^2 + 2\\text{Re}(z_1 \\overline{z}_2) = 2r^2 + 2r^2 \\cos\\theta = \\lambda^2 r^2,\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\theta\\) is the angle between \\(z_1\\) and \\(z_2\\).\n4. Simplify to find \\(\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\lambda^2 - 2}{2}\\).\n5. Let \\(w = \\frac{z_1}{z_2}\\) (with \\(|w| = 1\\)), leading to the quadratic equation:  \n   \\[\n   w^2 + (2 - \\lambda^2)w + 1 = 0.\n   \\]\n6. Verify that this quadratic holds under the derived \\(\\cos\\theta\\), confirming \\((z_1 + z_2)^2 = \\lambda^2 z_1 z_2\\).\n\n**Conclusion**: The equality \\((z_1 + z_2)^2 = \\lambda^2 z_1 z_2\\) holds if and only if \\(|z_1 + z_2| = \\lambda |z_k|\\) for \\(k = 1, 2\\).\n\n**Final Answer**  \nThe necessary and sufficient condition is \\(\\boxed{|z_1 + z_2| = \\lambda |z_k|}\\) for \\(k = 1\\) or \\(2\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Initial Problem Setup and Given Conditions:**\n   Given that \\( z_1 \\) and \\( z_2 \\) are two non-zero complex numbers with equal magnitudes, i.e., \\(\\lvert z_1 \\rvert = \\lvert z_2 \\rvert\\), we need to establish the condition such that:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left(z_1 + z_2\\right)^2 = \\lambda^2 z_1 z_2 \n   \\]\n\n   where \\( \\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}^+ \\).\n\n2. **Taking the Conjugate:**\n   To understand the necessity of the condition, take the complex conjugate of both sides:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\overline{z_1 + z_2}\\right)^2 = \\lambda^2 \\overline{z_1 z_2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Multiplying Both Expressions:**\n   Now, multiply the original equation by its conjugate:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left(z_1 + z_2\\right)^2 \\left(\\overline{z_1 + z_2}\\right)^2 = \\lambda^2 z_1 z_2 \\cdot \\lambda^2 \\overline{z_1 z_2}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\left|z_1 + z_2\\right|^4 = \\lambda^4 \\left|z_1 z_2\\right|^2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Using the Magnitude Property:**\n   We know \\(\\left|z_1 z_2 \\right| = \\left|z_1 \\right| \\left|z_2 \\right|\\). Given \\(\\left|z_1 \\right| = \\left|z_2 \\right|\\), denote this common magnitude by \\(\\left| z_k \\right|\\). Thus:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left| z_1 z_2 \\right| = \\left| z_k \\right|^2\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting this into the equation:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left| z_1 + z_2 \\right|^4 = \\lambda^4 \\left( \\left| z_k \\right|^2 \\right)^2 = \\lambda^4 \\left| z_k \\right|^4\n   \\]\n\n5. **Simplifying Magnitudes:**\n   Taking the fourth root on both sides of the equality:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left| z_1 + z_2 \\right| = \\lambda \\left| z_k \\right|\n   \\]\n\n    Since \\(\\lambda\\) is real and positive, this equation indeed imposes a necessary condition. For sufficiency, we will show the reverse implication below: \n\n6. **Additional Verification (Conjugates and Sufficient Condition):**\n   Given \\(\\left| z_1 \\right| = \\left| z_2 \\right| = \\left| z_k \\right|\\), we have:\n\n   \\[\n   \\overline{z_1} = \\frac{z_2 \\overline{z_2}}{z_1}\n   \\]\n\n    Hence,\n\n   \\[\n   \\overline{z_1 + z_2} = \\frac{\\overline{z_1} \\left( z_1+z_2 \\right)}{z_1}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Relation Between Magnitudes and Angles:**\n   Considering \\(\\left| z_1 + z_2 \\right| = \\lambda \\left| z_2 \\right|\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\left( z_1 + z_2 \\right) \\left( \\overline{z_1 + z_2} \\right) = \\lambda^2 z_2 \\overline{z_2}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore,\n\n   \\[\n   \\left( z_1 + z_2 \\right)^2 = \\lambda^2 z_1 z_2\n   \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nThus, we have shown both the necessary and the sufficient conditions for the given equation, and it is concluded that:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left| z_1 + z_2 \\right| = \\lambda \\left| z_k \\right| \\quad ( \\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}^+, k = 1, 2 )}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Little Rabbit and Little Turtle start from point $A$ to the Forest Amusement Park simultaneously. Little Rabbit jumps forward 36 meters in 1 minute and rests after every 3 minutes of jumping. The first rest period is 0.5 minutes, the second rest period is 1 minute, the third rest period is 1.5 minutes, and so on, with the $k$th rest period being $0.5k$ minutes. Little Turtle does not rest or play on the way. It is known that Little Turtle reaches the Forest Amusement Park 3 minutes and 20 seconds earlier than Little Rabbit. The distance from point $A$ to the Forest Amusement Park is 2640 meters. How many meters does Little Turtle crawl in 1 minute?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. So, the problem is about Little Rabbit and Little Turtle starting from point A to the Forest Amusement Park at the same time. The distance is 2640 meters. The turtle doesn't rest and arrives 3 minutes and 20 seconds earlier than the rabbit. We need to find how many meters the turtle crawls in one minute.\n\nFirst, I need to figure out the total time each of them took. Since the turtle arrives earlier, its time is less than the rabbit's time by 3 minutes and 20 seconds. Let me convert that time into minutes to make it easier. 3 minutes and 20 seconds is 3 + 20/60 = 3 + 1/3 \u2248 3.333... minutes. So, if T_rabbit is the total time the rabbit took, then the turtle's time T_turtle = T_rabbit - 10/3 minutes (since 3.333... is 10/3). Wait, actually, 3 minutes 20 seconds is 3 + 20/60 = 3 + 1/3 = 10/3 minutes. Yes, so T_turtle = T_rabbit - 10/3.\n\nSince the turtle doesn't rest, its total time is just the distance divided by its speed. Let's denote the turtle's speed as v meters per minute. Then, T_turtle = 2640 / v.\n\nFor the rabbit, it's more complicated because it has jumping periods and rest periods. The rabbit jumps 36 meters per minute, but it rests after every 3 minutes of jumping. The rest periods increase each time: first rest is 0.5 minutes, then 1 minute, then 1.5, etc., so the kth rest is 0.5k minutes.\n\nSo, the rabbit's journey is a series of cycles: jumping for 3 minutes, then resting for 0.5k minutes, where k is the cycle number. Each cycle consists of 3 minutes of jumping and a rest period. However, the last cycle might be incomplete if the rabbit reaches the destination before completing the 3 minutes of jumping.\n\nFirst, let's compute how much distance the rabbit covers in each cycle. Each 3-minute jumping period at 36 meters per minute. So, in 3 minutes, the rabbit jumps 36 * 3 = 108 meters. Each cycle (jumping + resting) adds 108 meters, but we need to check how many full cycles the rabbit completes before the final jump.\n\nTotal distance is 2640 meters. Let's see how many full cycles of 108 meters the rabbit does. 2640 divided by 108. Let's compute that: 108 * 24 = 2592, and 2640 - 2592 = 48 meters. So, 24 full cycles, which is 24 * 108 = 2592 meters, and then a final jump of 48 meters.\n\nWait, but each cycle is 3 minutes of jumping and then resting. So, each cycle's time is 3 minutes + rest time. The rest time for the kth cycle is 0.5k minutes. So, first cycle (k=1) rest is 0.5 minutes, second (k=2) rest is 1 minute, etc.\n\nSo, the total time for the rabbit is the time for the 24 full cycles plus the time for the final jump (which doesn't include a rest because it reaches the destination). Let's verify this.\n\nWait, 24 cycles would give 24*108 = 2592 meters. Then, remaining distance is 2640 - 2592 = 48 meters. To cover 48 meters at 36 meters per minute, the time needed is 48 / 36 = 1.333... minutes, which is 1 minute and 20 seconds. So, the total time for the rabbit is:\n\nTime for 24 cycles: each cycle has 3 minutes jumping + 0.5k minutes rest, where k is the cycle number. Wait, for each cycle, the rest period increases. So, first cycle: rest 0.5, second cycle rest 1, third rest 1.5, etc. So, the rest time for each cycle is 0.5k, where k is the cycle number. So, for the first cycle, rest after first 3 minutes is 0.5*1=0.5 minutes, second cycle rest is 0.5*2=1 minute, third cycle rest is 0.5*3=1.5 minutes, etc.\n\nTherefore, the total rest time after 24 cycles is the sum from k=1 to 24 of 0.5k. That is, 0.5 * sum(k=1 to 24 of k) = 0.5*(24*25)/2 = 0.5*300 = 150 minutes.\n\nWait, sum(k=1 to n) k is n(n+1)/2. So, sum(k=1 to 24) k = 24*25/2 = 300. Then, 0.5*300 = 150 minutes of rest.\n\nThen, the total jumping time for 24 cycles is 24*3 = 72 minutes. Then, the final 48 meters take 48/36 = 4/3 \u2248 1.333 minutes. So, total time for rabbit is 72 + 150 + 4/3 minutes. Wait, that can't be right. Wait, wait. Wait, each cycle is jumping for 3 minutes, then resting. So, for 24 cycles, that would be 24*3 minutes jumping + sum of rest times. Then, after the 24th rest, the rabbit starts the 25th cycle, jumps for 4/3 minutes to cover the remaining 48 meters, and doesn't rest because it's arrived. So, the total time is:\n\nJumping time: 24*3 + 4/3 = 72 + 1.333... = 73.333... minutes.\n\nRest time: sum from k=1 to 24 of 0.5k = 150 minutes.\n\nTotal time: 73.333... + 150 = 223.333... minutes. Which is 223 minutes and 20 seconds.\n\nBut the turtle took 223.333... - 10/3 minutes. Wait, 10/3 minutes is 3.333... minutes. So, 223.333... - 3.333... = 220 minutes. So, the turtle took 220 minutes.\n\nTherefore, the turtle's speed is 2640 meters / 220 minutes = 12 meters per minute.\n\nBut let me check again to make sure.\n\nWait, let's recast this step by step.\n\nFirst, rabbit's total distance: 2640 meters.\n\nRabbit's speed when jumping: 36 meters per minute.\n\nEach cycle: 3 minutes jumping (36*3=108 meters) followed by a rest period of 0.5k minutes, where k is the cycle number.\n\nNumber of full cycles needed: total distance / distance per cycle = 2640 / 108 \u2248 24.444... So, 24 full cycles give 24*108=2592 meters. Remaining distance: 2640 -2592=48 meters.\n\nTime taken for 24 full cycles: jumping time is 24*3=72 minutes. Rest time is sum of 0.5k from k=1 to 24. Sum of k from 1 to 24 is 24*25/2=300. So, rest time is 0.5*300=150 minutes.\n\nThen, after 24 cycles, the rabbit has 48 meters left. Time to jump that is 48/36=1.333... minutes (which is 1 minute 20 seconds). So, total jumping time: 72 + 1.333...=73.333... minutes. Total rest time:150 minutes. So, total rabbit time:73.333... +150=223.333... minutes, which is 223 minutes and 20 seconds.\n\nTurtle arrives 3 minutes and 20 seconds earlier, so turtle's time is 223.333... -3.333...=220 minutes. Therefore, turtle's speed is 2640 /220=12 meters per minute.\n\nWait, that seems straightforward, but let me verify again.\n\nWait, 24 cycles: each cycle, rest time is 0.5k. So, cycle 1 rest:0.5*1=0.5, cycle2:1, cycle3:1.5,..., cycle24:0.5*24=12 minutes. So total rest time is 0.5*(1+2+...+24)=0.5*(24*25)/2=0.5*300=150. Correct.\n\nTotal jumping time for 24 cycles:24*3=72. Plus the final 48 meters:48/36=1.333... So total jumping time:73.333... minutes. Rest time:150. Total time:73.333... +150=223.333... minutes. So, 223 minutes and 20 seconds. Turtle arrives 3 minutes and 20 seconds earlier, which is 223.333... -3.333...=220 minutes. So, 2640/220=12. Correct. So, answer is 12 meters per minute.\n\nBut let me check if the cycles are correctly accounted. The rabbit does 24 cycles, each with 3 minutes jumping and increasing rest. Then, after the 24th rest, it starts the 25th cycle, jumps for 1.333 minutes, reaches the destination, and doesn't rest. Therefore, the rest periods are only after each completed 3-minute jump. Since the last jump is less than 3 minutes, there's no rest after it. Therefore, the rest periods are exactly 24, each after a completed 3-minute jump. So, the calculation is correct.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there is a miscalculation here. Let me think again.\n\nSuppose the rabbit does the following: jumps 3 minutes, rests 0.5k minutes, where k is the cycle number. Each cycle consists of jumping and resting. But the cycle count is determined by the number of rests. So, if he completes 24 cycles, that would mean 24 rests. But each cycle is jump then rest, so after 24 cycles, he has done 24 jumps and 24 rests, and then does a final jump. Wait, no. Wait, the cycle is: jump 3 minutes, then rest. So, if he does 24 cycles, that's 24 jumps and 24 rests, totaling 24*(3 +0.5k) minutes? Wait no, wait. Wait, each cycle's rest time is 0.5k, where k is the cycle number. So, first cycle: jump 3, rest 0.5*1. Second cycle: jump 3, rest 0.5*2. Third cycle: jump 3, rest 0.5*3. So, the rest time for cycle k is 0.5k. Therefore, total rest time is sum from k=1 to 24 of 0.5k. Which is 0.5*(1+2+...+24)=0.5*300=150.\n\nBut the total jumping time is 24 cycles *3 minutes=72. Then, after 24 cycles, he has 2592 meters. Then, he needs 48 more meters. So, time for that is 48/36=1.333... minutes. So, that's an additional jump time without a rest. Therefore, total time is 72 +150 +1.333...=223.333... minutes. So, yes, correct.\n\nTherefore, the turtle's time is 223.333... -3.333...=220 minutes. So, 2640/220=12. So, answer is 12.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I need to check if the rest periods are correctly placed.\n\nWait, for example, after the first 3 minutes of jumping, he rests 0.5 minutes. Then, another 3 minutes jumping, rests 1 minute. Then, 3 minutes jumping, rests 1.5 minutes, etc. So, each rest period is after each 3-minute jump. Therefore, if he does 24 cycles (i.e., 24 rests), that would correspond to 24 jumping periods of 3 minutes each, totaling 72 minutes, and rests totaling 150 minutes. Then, after the 24th rest, he starts the 25th jump, which only needs 1.333... minutes to finish, so no rest after that. So, total time is 72 +150 +1.333...=223.333... minutes. Correct.\n\nYes, this seems accurate. Therefore, the turtle's speed is 12 meters per minute.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe Little Turtle crawls \\boxed{12} meters in 1 minute.", "deepseek_solution": "Little Rabbit and Little Turtle start from point \\( A \\) to the Forest Amusement Park simultaneously. The distance is 2640 meters. Little Turtle arrives 3 minutes and 20 seconds (which is \\( \\frac{10}{3} \\) minutes) earlier than Little Rabbit. We need to determine the turtle's speed.\n\n1. **Rabbit's Journey Analysis:**\n   - Rabbit jumps 36 meters per minute.\n   - Jumps for 3 minutes followed by a rest period. The \\( k \\)-th rest period is \\( 0.5k \\) minutes.\n   - Total distance: 2640 meters.\n\n2. **Rabbit's Cycles and Rest Periods:**\n   - Each cycle: 3 minutes jumping (108 meters) + rest period.\n   - Number of full cycles: \\( \\frac{2640}{108} \\approx 24.44 \\), so 24 full cycles (2592 meters) and a remaining 48 meters.\n   - Rest time for 24 cycles: \\( 0.5 \\times \\sum_{k=1}^{24} k = 0.5 \\times \\frac{24 \\times 25}{2} = 150 \\) minutes.\n   - Jumping time for 24 cycles: \\( 24 \\times 3 = 72 \\) minutes.\n   - Time for remaining 48 meters: \\( \\frac{48}{36} = \\frac{4}{3} \\) minutes (1 minute 20 seconds).\n\n3. **Total Time for Rabbit:**\n   - Total jumping time: \\( 72 + \\frac{4}{3} = 73.333 \\) minutes.\n   - Total rest time: 150 minutes.\n   - Total time: \\( 73.333 + 150 = 223.333 \\) minutes (223 minutes 20 seconds).\n\n4. **Turtle's Time:**\n   - Turtle arrives 3 minutes 20 seconds earlier: \\( 223.333 - \\frac{10}{3} = 220 \\) minutes.\n   - Turtle's speed: \\( \\frac{2640}{220} = 12 \\) meters per minute.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Calculate the time the rabbit travels without resting:**\n\nThe rabbit jumps 36 meters in 1 minute and travels a total distance of 2640 meters.\n\nTo find the number of jumps needed:\n\\[\nn = \\frac{2640}{36} = 73 \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\nThus, the rabbit makes 73 full jumps and an additional \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) of a jump.\n\n2. **Determine the number of times the rabbit rests:**\n\nSince the rabbit rests every 3 jumps:\n\\[\n\\text{Number of rests} = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{73}{3} \\right\\rfloor = 24 \\text{ times}\n\\]\nThe sequence of rest times is given by \\(0.5, 1, 1.5, \\cdots, 12\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the total resting time:**\n\nThe sequence of rest times is an arithmetic sequence:\n\\[\na = 0.5, \\quad l = 12, \\quad n = 24\n\\]\nThe sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[\nS_n = \\frac{n}{2} (a + l)\n\\]\nSubstituting in the values:\n\\[\nS_{24} = \\frac{24}{2} (0.5 + 12) = 12 \\times 12.5 = 150 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\n4. **Calculate the total time the rabbit spends traveling (including resting):**\n\nThe rabbit takes 73 minutes and \\(\\frac{1}{3}\\) for jumping and 150 minutes for resting:\n\\[\n\\text{Total time of rabbit} = 73 + \\frac{1}{3} + 150 = 223 \\frac{1}{3} \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\n5. **Determine the time the turtle spends traveling:**\n\nWe know that the turtle arrives 3 minutes and 20 seconds (3.333 minutes) earlier:\n\\[\n\\text{Time of turtle} = 223 \\frac{1}{3} - 3 \\frac{1}{3} = 220 \\text{ minutes}\n\\]\n\n6. **Calculate the turtle's speed:**\n\nThe total distance is 2640 meters and the time is 220 minutes:\n\\[\n\\text{Turtle's speed} = \\frac{2640 \\text{ meters}}{220 \\text{ minutes}} = 12 \\text{ meters per minute}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Vika filled a \\( 3 \\times 3 \\) grid with numbers from 1 to 9 such that the sum of any two numbers in adjacent cells is less than 12. The mischievous Andrey erased all the even numbers: 2, 4, 6, and 8.\n\nHelp Vika restore the grid by determining which number was in each cell.\n\nGiven the grid:\n\n| $A$ | $\\mathbf{1}$ | $\\mathbf{9}$ |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| $\\mathbf{3}$ | $\\mathbf{5}$ | $D$ |\n| $B$ | $C$ | $\\mathbf{7}$ |\n\nDetermine the correspondence:\n\n- In the square $A$\n- In the square $B$\n- In the square $C$\n- In the square $D$\n- the number 2 was.\n- the number 4 was.\n- the number 6 was.\n- the number 8 was.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out where the even numbers (2, 4, 6, 8) were in Vika's 3x3 grid before Andrey erased them. The grid has some numbers already filled in, and we need to place the erased even numbers into the empty cells A, B, C, D. The key constraint is that the sum of any two adjacent numbers (including diagonally adjacent?) must be less than 12. Wait, the problem says \"adjacent cells\" \u2013 usually adjacent means sharing a side, not diagonally. So I should consider only horizontal and vertical neighbors, not diagonal. Let me confirm that. Yes, in most grid problems, adjacent means sharing a side unless specified otherwise. So diagonals don't count here.\n\nFirst, let me visualize the grid as given:\n\nTop row: A | 1 | 9\n\nMiddle row: 3 | 5 | D\n\nBottom row: B | C | 7\n\nSo the cells we need to fill are A (top-left), B (bottom-left), C (bottom-middle), D (top-right of the middle row). The even numbers to place are 2, 4, 6, 8.\n\nWe need to assign 2,4,6,8 to A, B, C, D such that every pair of adjacent cells (sharing a side) sum to less than 12.\n\nLet's note the existing numbers and their neighbors:\n\nStarting with cell A (top-left). Its neighbors are the cell to the right (which is 1) and the cell below (which is 3). So whatever number is in A must satisfy:\n\nA + 1 < 12 \u21d2 A < 11 (which is always true since the numbers are 1-9)\n\nA + 3 < 12 \u21d2 A < 9. Since A is an even number (2,4,6,8), so possible values for A: 2,4,6,8. But wait, A must be less than 9, but since A is already an even number between 2-8, all are allowed. So no constraint from that.\n\nBut wait, maybe other constraints? Let's check cell A's neighbors. The cell to the right is 1, which is odd, so the sum of A and 1 must be less than 12. Similarly, the cell below A is 3, so A + 3 < 12. So A can be any even number (2,4,6,8) since even the largest possible A (8) plus 3 is 11, which is less than 12. So A can be 2,4,6,8. So no restriction here yet.\n\nNext, cell D (middle row right). Its neighbors are to the left (5) and below (7). So D must satisfy:\n\nD + 5 < 12 \u21d2 D < 7. Since D is an even number (2,4,6,8), possible D: 2,4,6. Because 8 would make D +5 = 13 which is \u226512, which violates the constraint. So D must be 2,4,6.\n\nSimilarly, cell D's right neighbor? Wait, D is in the middle row, rightmost cell. Its right neighbor would be outside the grid, so no. Its other neighbor is below it, which is 7. So D +7 <12 \u21d2 D <5. So D must be less than 5. But D is even, so possible D: 2,4. Because 6 +7=13 which is \u226512, so D can't be 6. Wait, but earlier we thought D could be 2,4,6. But now adding D +7 <12 \u21d2 D <5. So D must be 2 or 4. That's a stricter constraint.\n\nWait, let's check again. D is adjacent to 5 (left) and 7 (below). So the sum with 5 and with 7 must both be less than 12.\n\nSo D +5 <12 \u21d2 D <7 \u21d2 D=2,4,6\n\nBut D +7 <12 \u21d2 D <5 \u21d2 D=2,4\n\nTherefore, combining both, D must be 2 or 4.\n\nSo possible D: 2 or 4.\n\nThat's a key point. So D can only be 2 or 4. So we can note that.\n\nNow, moving to cell B (bottom-left). Its neighbors are above (3) and to the right (C). So B must satisfy:\n\nB +3 <12 \u21d2 B <9. Since B is even (2,4,6,8), all allowed.\n\nAlso, B + C <12. But C is another even number (2,4,6,8). So B and C must sum to less than 12.\n\nSimilarly, cell C (bottom-middle) is adjacent to B (left), 5 (above), and 7 (right). So:\n\nC +5 <12 \u21d2 C <7 \u21d2 C=2,4,6 (since 8 would make 8+5=13\u226512)\n\nC +7 <12 \u21d2 C <5 \u21d2 C=2,4\n\nBut wait, C is adjacent to 5 (above) and 7 (right). So both sums must be less than 12.\n\nTherefore:\n\nC +5 <12 \u21d2 C <7 \u21d2 C=2,4,6\n\nC +7 <12 \u21d2 C <5 \u21d2 C=2,4\n\nSo combining these, C must be 2 or 4.\n\nAlso, C is adjacent to B (left), so B + C <12. Since B is even (2,4,6,8), and C is 2 or 4.\n\nSo possible combinations for B and C:\n\nIf C=2, then B <10 (since B +2 <12 \u21d2 B <10). But B is even and \u22648, so B can be 2,4,6,8. But we need to check if B is available (since numbers are unique). But we have to assign 2,4,6,8 to A, B, C, D. If C is 2, then B can be 4,6,8 (but 2 is already used in C). Similarly, if C=4, then B +4 <12 \u21d2 B <8 \u21d2 B=2,4,6. But if C=4, then B 