m)\u22600, need to check if it's possible.\n\nAlternatively, take specific values.\n\nWe already have f(1)=1. Let's compute f(2). From m=2, 2f(2) -4 divides 8. So 2f(2) -4 |8. Let d=2f(2)-4. Then d divides 8 and d>0 (since f(2)\u22652, so 2*2 -4=0, but d=2f(2)-4 must be positive. So d>0. Hence, possible d=1,2,4,8. But d=2f(2)-4 must be even because 2f(2) is even and 4 is even. Therefore, d must be even. So possible d=2,4,8. Therefore:\n\nIf d=2: 2f(2)-4=2 \u21d22f(2)=6 \u21d2f(2)=3\n\nIf d=4: 2f(2)-4=4 \u21d22f(2)=8 \u21d2f(2)=4\n\nIf d=8: 2f(2)-4=8 \u21d22f(2)=12 \u21d2f(2)=6\n\nBut we saw that f(2)=3 fails for n=1, m=2. f(2)=4: check divisibility for m=2, n=1.\n\nf(2)=4. Then f(2)+f(1)-2*1=4+1-2=3. 3 divides 2*4 +1*1=8 +1=9. Yes, 3 divides 9. Good.\n\nNow check m=2, n=2. f(2)=4. Divisor=4+4 -4=4. Divides 2*4 +2*4=16. Yes, 4 divides 16.\n\nCheck m=2, n=3. Suppose f(3) is as per earlier possibilities. Let's assume f(3)=5 (from m=3, d=1: 2f(3)=9+1=10\u21d2f(3)=5). Then f(2)=4, f(3)=5. Check divisor=4+5 -6=3. Dividend=2*4 +3*5=8 +15=23. 3 divides 23? No. So even with f(2)=4, f(3)=5 fails.\n\nAlternatively, take f(3)=6. Then divisor=4+6 -6=4. Dividend=2*4 +3*6=8 +18=26. 4 divides 26? No, 26/4=6.5. No. So invalid.\n\nIf f(3)=9, divisor=4+9 -6=7. Dividend=8 +27=35. 7 divides 35. Yes. So f(3)=9 works with f(2)=4.\n\nSimilarly, check m=2, n=3 with f(3)=9. Then divisor=4+9 -6=7 divides 35. Yes. Now check m=3, n=1.\n\nf(3) +f(1) -3*1=9 +1 -3=7. Dividend=3*9 +1*1=27 +1=28. 7 divides 28. Yes.\n\nCheck m=3, n=3. Divisor=9 +9 -9=9. Divides 3*9 +3*9=54. Yes, 9 divides 54.\n\nCheck m=3, n=4. Suppose f(4)=9 (from earlier possibilities). Then divisor=9 +9 -12=6. Dividend=3*9 +4*9=27 +36=63. 6 divides 63? 63/6=10.5. No. So invalid. Therefore, even if f(3)=9 and f(4)=9, m=3,n=4 fails.\n\nThis suggests that even if we choose f(m)=m\u00b2 for m=1,2,3 but then choose a different f(4), it might fail. Therefore, perhaps the only function that works for all m,n is f(m)=m\u00b2.\n\nTo verify