0^{31}+3)\\) using polynomial division:**\n\n   Treat \\((10^{31})^3\\) as \\(a^3\\) where \\(a = 10^{31}\\), and apply polynomial division to divide \\(a^3\\) by \\(a + 3\\):\n\n   \\[\n   (10^{31})^3 = (10^{31})^3 = (a)^3 = a^3\n   \\]\n\n   We know from polynomial division:\n\n   \\[\n   a^3 = (a^2 - 3a + 9)(a + 3) - 27\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a^3}{a+3} = a^2 - 3a + 9 - \\frac{27}{a+3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a = 10^{31}\\) is extremely large, \\(\\frac{27}{a+3}\\) will be very small and can be ignored when taking the integer part. Thus we get:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left[\\frac{10^{93}}{10^{31}+3}\\right] \\approx (10^{31})^2 - 3(10^{31}) + 9\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the polynomial:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\left[\\frac{10^{93}}{10^{31}+3}\\right] \\approx 10^{62} - 3 \\cdot 10^{31} + 9\n   \\]\n\n4. **Extract only the significant terms and focus on the last two digits:**\n\n   Due to the massive size of \\(10^{62}\\) and \\(10^{31}\\), effectively the significant part which contributes to the last two digits will be:\n\n   \\[\n   (10^{31}) \\times (10^{31} - 3) + 8\n   \\]\n\n   Simplifying further by considering the last few digits:\n\n   \\[\n   \\left(10^{31} \\mod 100\\right) \\left(10^{31} - 3 \\mod 100\\right)\n   \\]\n\n   However, since \\(10^{31} \\equiv 0 \\mod 100\\):\n\n   \\textbf{Conclusion:}\n   The polynomial form does not alter the last two digits except the added small terms. Thus, taking into account the small remainder (i.e., 8), the resulting last two digits are:\n\n   \\(\\boxed{08}\\)\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\( \\boxed{08} \\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( x, y, z \\) be positive integers such that\n\n\\[ \n\\frac{6841x - 1}{9973} + \\frac{9973y - 1}{6841} = z \n\\]\n\nProve that\n\n\\[ \n\\frac{x}{9973} + \\frac{y}{6841} > 1 \n\\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that (x/9973) + (y/6841) > 1 given that (6841x - 1)/9973 + (99