' + c^2 y'^2) + (c x' - s y') = 1\\)\n\nNow, let's expand and collect like terms:\n\nFirst, expand each term:\n\n1. \\(2c^2 x'^2 - 4cs x'y' + 2s^2 y'^2\\)\n2. \\(3cs x'^2 + 3(c^2 - s^2) x'y' - 3cs y'^2\\)\n3. \\(s^2 x'^2 + 2sc x'y' + c^2 y'^2\\)\n4. \\(c x' - s y'\\)\n\nNow, sum all these terms:\n\nFor \\(x'^2\\) terms:\n\\(2c^2 + 3cs + s^2\\)\n\nFor \\(y'^2\\) terms:\n\\(2s^2 - 3cs + c^2\\)\n\nFor \\(x'y'\\) terms:\n\\(-4cs + 3(c^2 - s^2) + 2sc\\)\n\nFor \\(x'\\) terms:\n\\(c\\)\n\nFor \\(y'\\) terms:\n\\(-s\\)\n\nAnd the constant term is 0 on the left, equals 1 on the right.\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n[2c^2 + 3cs + s^2]x'^2 + [-4cs + 3(c^2 - s^2) + 2sc]x'y' + [2s^2 - 3cs + c^2]y'^2 + c x' - s y' = 1\n\\]\n\nOur goal is to eliminate the \\(x'y'\\) term. Therefore, we set the coefficient of \\(x'y'\\) to zero:\n\n\\(-4cs + 3(c^2 - s^2) + 2sc = 0\\)\n\nSimplify the terms:\n\nFirst, combine the \\(cs\\) terms: \\(-4cs + 2sc = -2cs\\)\n\nThen, the remaining term is \\(3(c^2 - s^2)\\). So:\n\n\\(-2cs + 3(c^2 - s^2) = 0\\)\n\nRecall that \\(c^2 - s^2 = \\cos 2\\theta\\) and \\(2cs = \\sin 2\\theta\\), but perhaps it's better to express in terms of \\(c\\) and \\(s\\). Alternatively, note that from earlier, we have \\( \\cot 2\\theta = \\frac{A - C}{B} = \\frac{2 - 1}{3} = 1/3 \\), so \\(2\\theta = \\cot^{-1}(1/3)\\). Alternatively, since \\(\\tan 2\\theta = 3\\), we can relate \\(c\\) and \\(s\\) through \\(2\\theta\\). \n\nAlternatively, since we know that \\( \\cot 2\\theta = (A - C)/B = 1/3 \\), so \\( \\cot 2\\theta = 1/3 \\), which implies \\( \\cos 2\\theta = 1/\\sqrt{10} \\) and \\( \\sin 2\\theta = 3/\\sqrt{10} \\), as we computed earlier. Then, since \\( \\cos 2\\theta = c^2 - s^2 = 1/\\sqrt{10} \\), and \\( \\sin 2\\theta = 2cs = 3/\\sqrt{10} \\). \n\nSo, let's use these relations. Let me denote \\( \\cos 2\\theta = 1/\\sqrt{10} \\), \\( \\sin 2\\theta = 3/\\sqrt{10} \\). Then, since \\( c^2 - s^2 = 1/\\sqrt{10} \\) and \\( 2cs = 3/\\sqrt{10} \\). \n\