:\n\n- Affects S2 and S3.\n\nSo each operation on pair (i, i+1) affects delta_i and delta_{i+1} (if we number the pairs as S1, S2, S3). Wait, maybe not exactly. Let's index the opposite sums as S1: sectors 1 and 4, S2: sectors 2 and 5, S3: sectors 3 and 6. Then, adding x to pair (1,2) affects S1 (sector1) and S2 (sector2). Adding x to pair (2,3) affects S2 (sector2) and S3 (sector3). Similarly, adding x to pair (3,4) affects S3 (sector3) and S1 (sector4, since sector4 is opposite sector1). Wait, no. Pair (3,4) is sectors3 and4. Sector4 is part of S1 (sectors1 and4). So adding x to pair (3,4) affects S3 (sector3) and S1 (sector4). Similarly, adding x to pair (4,5) affects S1 (sector4) and S2 (sector5). Pair (5,6) affects S2 (sector5) and S3 (sector6). Pair (6,1) affects S3 (sector6) and S1 (sector1). So each operation affects two of the three opposite sums. So the operations can adjust the delta for S1, S2, S3 in a linked way.\n\nThis seems similar to a system where the changes to S1, S2, S3 must satisfy certain conditions. Let me model this. Let\u2019s say we have variables representing the total change to each opposite sum:\n\nWe need delta1 = 2k - 0 = 2k\n\ndelta2 = 2k - 3\n\ndelta3 = 2k - 3\n\nBut the operations can only add some amount to pairs (S1,S2), (S2,S3), (S3,S1), etc., depending on the pair. Wait, actually, each operation corresponds to adding x to two adjacent opposite sums. For example, adding x to pair (1,2) adds x to S1 and x to S2. Similarly, adding x to pair (2,3) adds x to S2 and x to S3. Adding x to pair (3,4) adds x to S3 and x to S1. Similarly, pair (4,5) adds x to S1 and S2, pair (5,6) adds x to S2 and S3, pair (6,1) adds x to S3 and S1. Wait, actually, each pair (i,i+1) affects S_i and S_{i+1}, where the indices wrap around modulo 3. Wait, perhaps not exactly. Let me check:\n\nWait, when you add x to pair (1,2):\n\n- Sector1 (part of S1) gets +x\n\n- Sector2 (part of S2) gets +x\n\nTherefore, delta1 += x, delta2 +=x\n\nSimilarly, adding x to 