cal properties, we have:\n- \\( \\angle DCT = \\alpha \\)\n- \\( \\angle MCE = \\beta \\)\n\nNow, consider the cyclic quadrilateral formed by points \\( C, T, K, B \\). According to the inscribed angle theorem, we know:\n\\[ \\angle TKC = \\angle TBC = \\alpha \\]\nSimilarly, for quadrilateral \\( PKMC \\):\n\\[ \\angle CKM = \\angle CAM = \\beta \\]\n\nAdditionally, since \\( \\angle DPT = \\angle DCT = \\alpha \\) and \\( \\angle EPM = \\angle ECM = \\beta \\), it follows:\n\n\\[\n\\angle TKM + \\angle TPM = (\\alpha + \\beta) + (180^\\circ - \\alpha - \\beta) = 180^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, by the property that opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum up to \\( 180^\\circ \\), we conclude that points \\( P, M, K, T \\) are concyclic.\n\nTherefore, the perpendiculars from \\( C \\) to \\( DE \\), \\( EA \\), \\( AB \\), and \\( BD \\) are concurrent at the orthocenter of \\( \\triangle PMKT \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The perpendiculars from \\( C \\) to \\( DE \\), \\( EA \\), \\( AB \\), and \\( BD \\) are concurrent.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In triangle \\( \\triangle ABC \\), if \\( 2 \\sin A \\cos B = \\sin C \\), then the triangle is:\n(A) Isosceles.\n(B) Right.\n(C) Scalene.\n(D) Equilateral.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, let me try to figure out this trigonometry problem. So, the question is about triangle ABC, and we're given the equation 2 sin A cos B = sin C. We need to determine what type of triangle it is\u2014whether it's isosceles, right, scalene, or equilateral. The options are A through D. Let me start by recalling some trigonometric identities and triangle properties that might help here.\n\nFirst, I remember that in any triangle, the sum of the angles is \u03c0 radians (or 180 degrees). So, A + B + C = \u03c0. Maybe that can help relate the angles somehow. Also, the Law of Sines says that a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C = 2R, where R is the radius of the circumscr