\\),\n   - \\( S_2 \\) as the area of \\( \\triangle BDC \\).\n\n2. It is given that:\n   \\[\n   S = S_1 + S_2\n   \\]\n   because the area of \\( \\triangle ABC \\) is the sum of the areas of \\( \\triangle ABD \\) and \\( \\triangle BDC \\).\n\n3. We know that the area of a triangle can be represented by its semiperimeter and the radius of its inscribed circle:\n   \\[\n   S = p \\cdot r\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   S_1 = p_1 \\cdot r_1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   S_2 = p_2 \\cdot r_2\n   \\]\n\n4. By substituting the area expressions, we get:\n   \\[\n   p \\cdot r = p_1 \\cdot r_1 + p_2 \\cdot r_2\n   \\]\n\n5. To find \\( r \\), solve for \\( r \\) in terms of \\( r_1 \\) and \\( r_2 \\):\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{p_1 \\cdot r_1 + p_2 \\cdot r_2}{p}\n   \\]\n\n6. Considering the properties of the triangle:\n   - The semiperimeter \\( p_1 \\) of \\( \\triangle ABD \\) is less than \\( p \\), because \\( BD < BC + CD \\),\n   - Similarly, the semiperimeter \\( p_2 \\) of \\( \\triangle BDC \\) is also less than \\( p \\).\n\n7. Thus:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{p_1}{p} < 1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{p_2}{p} < 1\n   \\]\n\n8. Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{p_1 \\cdot r_1}{p} < r_1 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{p_2 \\cdot r_2}{p} < r_2\n   \\]\n\n9. Adding these inequalities gives:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{p_1 \\cdot r_1}{p} + \\frac{p_2 \\cdot r_2}{p} < r_1 + r_2\n   \\]\n\n10. Thus:\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{p_1 \\cdot r_1 + p_2 \\cdot r_2}{p} < r_1 + r_2\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{r_1 + r_2 > r}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the smallest positive integer \\( n > 1 \\) such that the arithmetic mean of the squares of the integers \\( 1^2, 2^2, 3^2, \\ldots, n^2 \\) is a perfect square.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this problem. We need to find the smallest positive integer n > 1 such that the arithmetic mean of the squares from 1\u00b2 to n\u00b2 is a perfect square. Hmm, let's